    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "depth": 0,
      "kind": "section",
      "number": "1",
      "title": "Definition",
      "body": "● Klappvisor Bascinet is a medieval helmet that originated in the 14th century.\n\"Klappvisor\" is German and translates to \"folding visor.\" The single brow-mounted hinge is a distinctive feature of early bascinet visor designs. These bascinet visors were often low profile to fit underneath a great helm, but would also serve as a primary defense on their own.\n● Klappvisor uses a solid metal visor to protect the face, attached on the forehead by a piece of metal, the “spoon”.\n● Klappvisor was used from 1340 to 1400, mainly in Germany and Italy.\n● Klappvisor is approved for 14th century and Transitional armors.\n● Klappvisor is denied for 15th century armors.",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
      "stable_key": "klappvisors:1",
